Source package: glib2.0

scope-before-exec.patch
    Puts each application which GDesktopAppInfo launches into its own systemd scope before the
    application starts, app-gnome-<application ID>-<pid>.scope, so that every process of the
    application starts in that scope. GNOME Shell, gnome-session and gnome-settings-daemon asked
    systemd for the scope only once GLib had started the application, and systemd moves only the
    process it is given, so the processes the application started in the meantime stayed in the
    cgroup of the service which launched it, and were stopped with that service. Signal's zygotes
    and GPU process stayed in gnome-session-manager@ubuntu.service, and Thunderbird's crash helper
    in org.gnome.Shell@ubuntu.service.

    GLib sends the request right after it spawned gio-launch-desktop, before the launch call
    returns, and gio-launch-desktop, which executes the application, first waits until
    /proc/self/cgroup names the scope. If it ran the application before that, every process the
    application starts until systemd moves it would stay outside of the scope, which the patch is
    there to prevent. gio-launch-desktop therefore waits as long as systemd works on the request:
    the launching process follows the request to its end, and writes the reason to a pipe to
    gio-launch-desktop when systemd refused it, or when the job starting the scope ended with
    another result than done. When systemd is busy, an application therefore starts late, but whole.
    gio-launch-desktop gives up only when nothing can tell it the outcome, because the launching
    process exited before systemd finished, as gio open does, or never runs its main loop again. It
    then gives up after 60 seconds, which systemd needs for a request only when it is badly stuck.

    GLib asks for no scope where systemd would refuse it for certain: when systemd is not on the
    session bus, as on a test's dbus-run-session, which GLib checks with the bus before spawning,
    and when the name is longer than systemd accepts. Starting an application waits about 25 ms
    longer for the scope, and the launch call itself returns a few milliseconds later, as it waits
    until the request is sent. A launching process which never runs its main loop again keeps the
    write end of each pipe open.

    An application which runs outside of its scope after all is logged at error priority, which the
    daily journal digest reports: as "gio-launch-desktop: systemd did not create scope ...", with
    the reason the launching process passes on over the pipe, and as "gio-launch-desktop: Scope ...
    did not exist after 60 seconds". Neither is expected. A refusal with
    org.freedesktop.systemd1.TransactionIsDestructive, which systemd answers while it stops at the
    end of a session, is logged at notice priority. A journal stream gives every line the same
    priority, info for an application GNOME Shell launches, so when standard error is the journal,
    gio-launch-desktop logs through syslog instead, and otherwise to standard error.

    systemd looks up a process ID in its own PID namespace, so a launching process in a sandbox
    with a PID namespace of its own, such as Firejail's, which still uses the session bus, would
    name some other process. GLib therefore gives systemd the process as a pidfd, when the bus can
    pass one. gio-launch-desktop counts a cgroup below the scope's as in the scope, as Fedora's
    uresourced moves each process of an application into one.

    Processes started without GDesktopAppInfo are not covered by this patch. GNOME Shell's commands,
    run with Alt+F2 or by its spawn helpers, and gnome-settings-daemon's fallback Orca are therefore
    launched through GDesktopAppInfo as well, by patches/gnome-shell/spawn-commands-in-scope.patch
    and patches/gnome-settings-daemon/orca-fallback-in-scope.patch. Helpers which GNOME Shell spawns
    with pipes to talk to, like the authentication dialogs of VPN plugins, are not applications, and
    stay where they are.

    The scope is requested only by a process managed by the systemd user instance, and not in a
    Flatpak or a snap. The name is the one gnome-desktop's gnome_start_systemd_scope() gives the
    scope, so gnome-session's /usr/lib/systemd/user/app-gnome-.scope.d/ applies, and a launching
    process which still asks for the scope afterwards fails without moving anything, while
    gnome_start_systemd_scope() logs a warning about it. The "launched" signal names the scope as
    "systemd-unit" in its platform data, which the patches of the same name in patches/gnome-shell/,
    patches/gnome-session/ and patches/gnome-settings-daemon/ look for to leave the scope to GLib.
    A snap's command, snap run, moves itself into a scope of its own before it executes the snap,
    which is after GLib's scope, so the snap still ends up in its own.

    The approach follows https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/1596, without its
    shell wrapper, as gio-launch-desktop is back in the launch since GLib 2.86. GLib's developers
    prepared GDesktopAppInfo for scopes in https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/5350
    (merged after 2.88.0), with the scopes themselves announced as a follow-up. Drop this patch,
    and the three which depend on it, once GLib creates scopes itself, and check then which name and
    which platform data key it uses.

    The request is sent in g_desktop_app_info_launch_uris_with_spawn in gio/gdesktopappinfo.c, and
    the wait is wait_for_scope in gio/gio-launch-desktop.c. After a version bump, check that
    launching still spawns gio-launch-desktop and emits "launched" only after the spawn.

    The patch adds two tests to gio/tests/desktop-app-info.c, launch-desktop/scope-refused and
    launch-desktop/scope-pending, for the wait of gio-launch-desktop. The build runs outside of a
    systemd user instance, so the scope itself is checked by tests/run-scope-test.sh, in a
    container with a user instance of its own, see tests/README.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login:

      systemd-cgls --user-unit gnome-session-manager@ubuntu.service
                                  should list gnome-session-service alone
      systemd-cgls --user-unit org.gnome.Shell@ubuntu.service
                                  should list gnome-shell, Xwayland and mutter-x11-frames alone,
                                  also after starting Thunderbird or Signal from the dock
      journalctl --user -b | grep 'Could not create transient scope'
                                  should find nothing
